threads.bodyText:
Summary
@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot resolves "the running dsh version" from its own package.json. A plugin bundle that mounts a harness package (for example @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry) gets its own nested copy of app-boot. When that copy is a different version from the launcher, prepareProfileEntries() checks plugins against the wrong runtime version and disables plugins that are compatible with the launcher that actually runs.
Related family, different mechanism: #10364, #10338. Those are about old core packages that a plugin installs. Here all packages are at the correct version; only the version that app-boot reports is wrong.
Environment

dsh launcher: 0.2.1-rc.2 (npm latest), macOS 27, Node 26.8.1
Plugin bundle: @sagmans/dsh-tui 0.15.0, which mounts @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry@0.2.0-rc.2 (so it carries dsh-app-boot@0.2.0-rc.2)

Actual behavior
On dsh --profile tui start, stderr shows:
dsh: disabling profile plugin row "tool-presentation": Plugin
@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-tool-presentation@0.2.1-rc.2 is incompatible with dsh
0.2.0-rc.2: peerDependencies {"@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools":"0.2.1-rc.2"}. ...

The launcher is 0.2.1-rc.2, so the plugin is compatible. The message names 0.2.0-rc.2, which is the version of the nested app-boot copy. Result: PTC run_code cards lose their tool presentation (no title).
The failure mirrors in the other direction: a bundle that pins 0.2.1-rc.2, run on a 0.2.0-rc.2 launcher, disables dsh-agent-tool-presentation@0.2.0-rc.2 the same way. So a bundle that mounts harness packages works fully on exactly one harness release.
Expected behavior
Plugin compatibility is checked against the version of the launcher that runs, whichever app-boot copy performs the check.
Minimal repro (no profile, no model)
Needs one app-boot 0.2.0-rc.2 copy and one dsh 0.2.1-rc.2 install.
// runtime-version.mjs
// Usage: node runtime-version.mjs <nested app-boot dir> <launcher app-boot dir> <plugin dir>
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'
const [nested, launcher, plugin] = process.argv.slice(2)
const load = dir => import(pathToFileURL(join(dir, 'lib/index.js')).href)
const manifest = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(plugin, 'package.json'), 'utf8'))
const a = await load(nested)
const b = await load(launcher)
const verdict = r => r === undefined ? 'compatible' : 'INCOMPATIBLE ' + JSON.stringify(r)
console.log('launcher app-boot runtime:', b.getDshRuntimeVersion(), '->', verdict(b.evaluatePluginCompatibility(manifest)))
console.log('nested app-boot runtime:', a.getDshRuntimeVersion(), '->', verdict(a.evaluatePluginCompatibility(manifest)))
node runtime-version.mjs \
  <bundle>/node_modules/.pnpm/@deepseek-ai+dsh-app-boot@0.2.0-rc.2*/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot \
  <dsh-0.2.1-rc.2>/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot \
  <dsh-0.2.1-rc.2>/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-tool-presentation

Output:
launcher app-boot runtime: 0.2.1-rc.2 -> compatible
nested app-boot runtime: 0.2.0-rc.2 -> INCOMPATIBLE {"name":"@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-tool-presentation","version":"0.2.1-rc.2","runtimeVersion":"0.2.0-rc.2","peers":{"@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools":"0.2.1-rc.2"},"exempted":false}

Root cause
dsh-app-boot/lib/index.js (0.2.0-rc.2 and 0.2.1-rc.2):
function getDshRuntimeVersion() {
  const filename = fileURLToPath(new URL("../package.json", import.meta.url));
  ...
  return runtimeVersionOf(manifest.version);
}
function evaluatePluginCompatibility(manifest, exemptions = {}, runtimeVersion = getDshRuntimeVersion()) { ... }
prepareProfileEntries(ctx, entries, parentURL) calls the preflight without a runtime version, so each app-boot copy answers with its own package version. The launcher's version is known, but it is not passed through ctx.
Suggested fix

Publish the launcher's runtime version in the profile context (or as a service), and make prepareProfileEntries / evaluatePluginCompatibility use it before the package-local fallback.
Or: when a nested app-boot copy differs from the launcher, fail with an error that names both versions, so the mismatch is explicit.
